from pathlib import Path
import sys
sys.path.insert(0,str(Path(__file__).resolve().parents[2]/'authoring'))
from engine import Author
A=Author('19');add=A.add
add('4.3.4-01','In increasing frequency, the school spectrum order is radio, microwave, infrared, visible, ultraviolet, X-ray, gamma.','Wavelength decreases along that order for a common vacuum speed.','These names divide a continuous spectrum into useful regions. Since c = fλ, a higher frequency at fixed speed requires a shorter wavelength. Learn the order with an explicit direction rather than memorising a sequence without knowing which end is higher. Boundaries between adjacent named bands are conventions rather than physical gaps.','Explain how frequency and wavelength change from radio toward gamma radiation in vacuum.','Link the order radio, microwave, infrared, visible, ultraviolet, X-ray, gamma to increasing frequency.~Because the vacuum speed is common, wavelength decreases in that direction.')
regions=[('M01','Radio waves','the low-frequency, long-wavelength end relative to the other named bands','Microwaves lie next in the stated increasing-frequency order.','A radio transmitter uses a selected frequency rather than emitting an undifferentiated whole spectrum. A longer wavelength does not make it a sound wave.','State the named region at the lowest-frequency end of the school electromagnetic spectrum.','Radio waves.'),('M02','Microwaves','between radio and infrared in the school sequence','They have higher frequency and shorter wavelength than the radio region listed before them.','Microwaves are often treated as a radio-frequency subregion in wider classifications. In this course sequence they have a separate label for communication and heating applications.','State the two regions neighbouring microwaves in the school spectrum sequence.','Radio and infrared.'),('M03','Infrared','between microwaves and visible light','Its frequency is below visible red and its wavelength is longer.','Infrared is invisible to the ordinary human eye. A warm object can emit infrared without glowing visibly red. Its thermal applications depend on absorption and emission.','State where infrared lies relative to visible light in frequency.','Below visible light in frequency.'),('M04','Visible light','between infrared and ultraviolet','Within visible light, red has lower frequency and longer wavelength than violet.','The eye responds to a limited part of the electromagnetic spectrum. Visible is a response-based region, not the only radiation capable of transferring energy.','State the regions immediately below and above visible light in frequency.','Infrared below and ultraviolet above.'),('M05','Ultraviolet','between visible light and X-rays','It has higher frequency and shorter wavelength than visible violet.','Ultraviolet is outside ordinary vision. Different UV subregions have different absorption and biological effects; the school ordering is the broad comparison needed here.','State whether ultraviolet has higher or lower frequency than visible light.','Higher frequency.'),('M06','X-rays','between ultraviolet and gamma in the school sequence','They have short wavelengths and high frequencies.','X-ray and gamma energy ranges can overlap in detailed classifications, where origin distinguishes the names. The syllabus ordering describes their typical regions and should not be treated as a claim of perfectly sharp universal boundaries.','State the named region between ultraviolet and gamma in the school order.','X-rays.'),('M07','Gamma rays','the high-frequency, short-wavelength end of the school sequence','They are electromagnetic radiation, not helium nuclei or electrons.','Gamma radiation is associated with nuclear transitions in the basic nuclear account. Its high penetration is useful for certain imaging and industrial tasks but creates exposure concerns.','State the region at the highest-frequency end of the school spectrum order.','Gamma rays.')]
for k,name,position,detail,teach,prompt,answer in regions:add('4.3.4-01-'+k,name+' occupy '+position+'.',detail,teach,prompt,answer)
add('4.3.4-02','The named electromagnetic waves in this spectrum are transverse and travel in vacuum at approximately 3.0 × 10⁸ m/s.','Their frequencies and wavelengths differ despite this common vacuum speed.','They can cross a vacuum and do not require air. In a material their speeds can depend on the material and wavelength; the vacuum statement must not be copied unqualified to every substance. A radio signal and a visible-light signal travelling the same vacuum distance take equal propagation times in this model.','State the approximate speed of electromagnetic radiation in vacuum.','3.0 × 10⁸ m/s.',heading='Shared electromagnetic properties')
add('4.3.4-03','Electromagnetic applications depend on propagation, detection, absorption and penetration.','The useful interaction determines the selected band rather than frequency alone being a universal measure of suitability.','Communication uses a controlled signal that can be transmitted and received. Imaging can exploit different absorption through a material. Heating uses absorbed energy, while sterilisation or treatment uses radiation’s biological effects under controlled conditions. Match the named application to its mechanism rather than giving only a band name.','Explain why radio broadcasting and X-ray imaging use different electromagnetic interactions.','Link radio to carrying a signal detected by a receiver.~X-ray imaging uses differential transmission through materials to reveal internal structure.')
add('4.3.4-03-M01','Radio communication transmits information by controlled changes in an electromagnetic carrier.','A receiver detects the signal and recovers its information.','An aerial emits and receives the selected radio signal. Sound from a broadcast microphone is converted into an electrical information signal; it does not itself travel through space as a sound wave to the receiving radio. The receiver converts the recovered information into sound if required.','Explain how a radio broadcast can carry speech without sound travelling through the whole path.','The speech information controls a radio-frequency electromagnetic signal.~A receiver recovers it and a loudspeaker produces local sound.')
add('4.3.4-03-M02','Microwaves can carry information between suitable transmitters, receivers and satellites.','A directed beam and suitable receiving equipment support a communication link.','The link sends encoded information electromagnetically, rather than transporting an object through the air. Line of sight and alignment can matter. The same broad band has other applications, so describing microwave heating alone does not explain its communication role.','Explain the role of microwaves in a satellite communication link.','A transmitter sends information on a controlled microwave signal.~The satellite and receiving equipment detect or relay it to complete the link.')
add('4.3.4-03-M03','Infrared can transfer heating energy or carry a coded control signal.','Warm-body emission can also be detected by suitable sensors.','A remote control emits pulses detected by its receiver. A thermal intruder detector senses changes in incoming infrared associated with a warm body. A heater instead relies on absorption that raises internal energy. These uses share a wave region but differ in whether the desired outcome is information or heating.','Explain how an infrared remote communicates a command.','It emits a coded pattern of infrared pulses.~The receiver detects the pattern and converts it into an electrical control signal.')
add('4.3.4-03-M04','Suitable ultraviolet radiation can damage microorganisms and is used in controlled sterilisation systems.','The same interaction can harm living tissue.','The educational link is between radiation absorption and damage to cellular material. Effective equipment must manage exposure, shielding and the treated region; this example does not supply a do-it-yourself dose or procedure. Do not confuse germ reduction by UV with merely warming a surface.','Explain why ultraviolet can be useful for sterilisation while requiring controlled exposure.','Absorbed ultraviolet can damage microorganisms.~It can also damage other living tissue, so exposure must be controlled.')
add('4.3.4-03-M05','X-ray imaging exploits different transmission through materials.','A detector records the spatial pattern of transmitted radiation.','In the simple bone-radiograph example, bone absorbs more strongly than surrounding soft tissue, so the detector receives different signals. Security imaging uses related material-dependent transmission. Radiograph contrast does not mean X-rays reflect from bones like light from a mirror. The examples describe physics rather than decisions about individual medical examinations.','Explain how X-rays reveal internal structures in an radiograph.','Different materials attenuate the incident radiation by different amounts.~The detector records the resulting transmission pattern as contrast.')
add('4.3.4-03-M06','Visible and infrared signals can be guided along optical fibres.','Total internal reflection in a suitable core-cladding system helps confine the light.','The core has higher refractive index than its surrounding cladding. Rays meeting the boundary under the required conditions remain guided. Coded pulses carry information; the glass is not moved along with the message. Real signal loss means repeaters or other system design may be needed over long paths.','Explain how an optical fibre guides a light signal.','A higher-index core and lower-index cladding permit total internal reflection under the appropriate angles.~Repeated confinement guides the coded light along the fibre.')
add('4.3.4-03-M07','Gamma radiation can affect cells and penetrate materials sufficiently for some industrial inspections.','Controlled treatment uses cell damage, while crack detection uses transmission differences.','In industrial radiography a defect changes the radiation reaching a detector, making internal structure visible. The same penetration that permits inspection requires exposure control. Cancer-treatment use is a syllabus example of a specialist application; it is not an instruction about individual treatment choice or dose.','Explain how gamma radiation can reveal a crack inside a metal component.','The crack changes the amount or path of attenuating material.~A detector records a transmission difference that can indicate the defect.')
add('4.3.4-04','Electromagnetic hazards depend on frequency, intensity, duration and the material exposed.','Heating can damage tissue, while sufficiently energetic radiation can cause ionisation and cellular damage.','Intense visible light can harm eyes; infrared and microwaves can cause excessive heating. Ultraviolet can damage skin and eyes, and X-rays and gamma rays are ionising. A useful answer names a mechanism and affected tissue rather than calling every band equally dangerous under every exposure. Lower photon energy does not imply unlimited intensity is harmless.','Explain why both intense microwaves and X-rays can be hazardous through different mechanisms.','Microwave absorption can cause excessive tissue heating.~X-rays can ionise material and damage cells, so their exposure needs control.')
add('4.3.4-05','Microwave ovens use shielding and interlocks to contain operation, while X-ray systems control exposure with shielding and restricted access.','Equipment protection should not be bypassed.','A metal enclosure and designed door structure limit escaping microwave radiation. X-ray work uses appropriate barriers and exposure management because the beam can penetrate and ionise tissue. These are the intended safety principles in the syllabus, not repair or operating instructions for high-energy equipment.','State one important safety principle for a microwave oven.','Its shielding and door interlocks must remain functional and must not be bypassed.')
C=A.choice
C('4.3.4-01','Which sequence increases in frequency?',('Infrared, visible, ultraviolet','This is the correct neighbouring order.'),('Ultraviolet, visible, infrared','This decreases in frequency.'),('Visible, infrared, ultraviolet','The first step goes downward.'),('Infrared, ultraviolet, visible','The last step goes downward.'))
C('4.3.4-02','What do radio and gamma waves share in vacuum?',('Speed','Both travel at the common vacuum light speed.'),('Frequency','Their frequencies differ.'),('Wavelength','Their wavelengths differ.'),('Identical application','Their interactions lead to different uses.'))
C('4.3.4-03-M02','Which band is named for satellite telephone links in this course?',('Microwaves','They can carry the communication signal.'),('Sound','Sound cannot cross a vacuum.'),('Ocean waves','They do not provide that signal path.'),('Mechanical rope pulses','These require a rope.'))
C('4.3.4-03-M04','Which use is associated with ultraviolet?',('Controlled sterilisation','Its interaction can damage microorganisms.'),('Producing sound in a vacuum','That is not a UV role.'),('Measuring mass by buoyancy','That is unrelated.'),('Supplying a mechanical rope','It does not provide the stated medium.'))
C('4.3.4-03-M03','Which common controller uses infrared?',('A television remote','It sends coded infrared pulses.'),('A simple mechanical lever','It transfers force directly.'),('A water manometer','It measures pressure mechanically.'),('A plumb line','It uses gravity.'))
C('4.3.4-03-M06','What supports fibre guidance?',('Total internal reflection','Suitable core and cladding indices confine light.'),('Sound bouncing between water walls','This is not optical guidance.'),('Light needing air to exist','Light can travel through a vacuum.'),('Glass moving to the destination','The signal moves, not the fibre.'))
C('4.3.4-01-M04','Which visible colour has longer wavelength?',('Red compared with violet','Red has lower frequency in vacuum.'),('Violet compared with red','This reverses the relation.'),('Both have identical wavelength','Their frequencies and wavelengths differ.'),('Neither can be a wave','Both are electromagnetic radiation.'))
C('4.3.4-01','Vacuum frequency doubles. What happens to wavelength?',('It halves','The speed remains c = fλ.'),('It doubles','That would quadruple the product.'),('It stays fixed','Then speed would change.'),('It becomes zero','A finite frequency gives finite wavelength.'))
C('4.3.4-03-M05','A region absorbs more X-rays. What happens to transmitted detector signal there?',('It decreases','Fewer photons reach the detector.'),('It increases from absorption alone','Absorption removes photons from that path.'),('It becomes a sound wave','The radiation type does not change this way.'),('It is unrelated to absorption','Differential absorption produces contrast.'))
C('4.3.4-03-M03','A warm body moves past an appropriate infrared detector. What can it detect?',('A change in infrared input','Warm-body emission supports detection.'),('Only visible colour','The detector responds to infrared.'),('The absence of all radiation','Warm bodies emit radiation.'),('The body’s mass directly','The signal is radiative, not a direct mass measurement.'))
C('4.3.4-05','An oven door interlock is faulty. Which principle follows?',('Do not bypass the protective interlock','It is part of containing operation.'),('Remove shielding to improve access','That defeats protection.'),('Assume microwaves are harmless at any intensity','They can heat tissue.'),('Use the fault as a source of extra cooking power','That is not the interlock’s role.'))
C('4.3.4-03-M07','Why can an internal crack change a radiograph?',('It changes attenuation along a path','The detector receives different transmitted radiation.'),('It makes gamma travel slower than sound','That is not the imaging mechanism.'),('It necessarily emits visible light','The detector uses transmitted radiation.'),('It removes the need for shielding','Exposure control still matters.'))
P=A.part
P('4.3.4-01-M05','Name the band just above visible in frequency.','Ultraviolet.',1,'One mark for ultraviolet.')
P('4.3.4-02','State the common vacuum electromagnetic speed.','3.0 × 10⁸ m/s.',1,'One mark for the stated speed and unit.')
P('4.3.4-03-M05','Explain X-ray radiograph contrast.','Different materials attenuate differently, so the detector receives a spatially varying transmitted signal.',2,'One mark for differing attenuation; one for detector contrast.')
P('4.3.4-03-M06','Explain why a fibre’s cladding should have lower index than its core.','This allows the travelling core light to approach a lower-index boundary. At sufficient incidence angle total internal reflection can confine it.',2,'One mark for index direction; one for reflection above critical angle.')
P('4.3.4-04','Explain why calling microwaves harmless because they are non-ionising is incomplete.','Absorption can still transfer substantial energy into tissue. Excessive heating can therefore cause damage.',2,'One mark for absorption/heating; one for the resulting hazard.')
